<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adat refers to the body of customary law and traditional practice in the Malay world and the Indonesian archipelago. It governed matters such as land, inheritance and social relations, often alongside religious law. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire</a>, local adat structures allowed communities to keep their own customs under the authority of the Javanese court.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adat.jpg" alt="Adat" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alberttito · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pengantin_Melayu_Deli.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word adat comes from the Arabic ʿādā́t, the plural of ʿā́da, meaning custom or habit, and is treated as a synonym of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urf">urf</a>, something commonly known or accepted. It describes local customary practices and traditions regarded as compatible with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> among Muslim communities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a>, and Central, South and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. In a broader sense it covers the norms, prohibitions and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a> that guide a person's conduct as a member of a community.</p><p>Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> arrived, the peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> already had long-established legal and civil codes, which in the Islamic period came to be called adat. In Central Asia councils of Aqsaqals, respected elders, guided it. In the North Caucasus the Teip, or clan, was the chief reference for loyalty, honour, shame and collective responsibility.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> the concept was first formulated in the Islamised Malay-speaking world, apparently to distinguish non-Islamic from Islamic practices. In the fifteenth century the Melaka empire produced a code of international shipping law, the Undang-Undang Laut Melaka, and a civil and commercial code, the Undang-Undang Melaka, heavily Islamic but with extensive adat influences. These later spread to sultanates such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a>, Pattani and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a>.</p><p>Malay society is divided into two traditions. Adat temenggung is patrilineal and found in most Malay states. Adat perpatih is matrilineal, tied to a kinship-based political structure, and confined almost entirely to Negeri Sembilan, where descendants of Minangkabau immigrants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-sumatra">West Sumatra</a> live. Malay culture also distinguishes several kinds of adat, among them adatullah, the natural order created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a>, and adat muhakamah, the laws that rulers compiled into legal codes, often blended with Islamic law. The earliest example of the latter is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terengganu-inscription-stone">Terengganu Inscription Stone</a> of 1303, which sets out ten basic laws.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In the early twentieth century the study of adat became a specialised field in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>. Scholars such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornelis-van-vollenhoven">Cornelis van Vollenhoven</a>, Ter Haar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christiaan-snouck-hurgronje">Christiaan Snouck Hurgronje</a> compared its systems, and the colonial government used adatrecht as a legal term. The customs of all ethnic groups, non-Muslims included, were collectively called adat, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> divided the East Indies into at least nineteen adat law areas.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, the colonial administration left local legal practice to councils of aqsaqals and teips, as did the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> in the first years after 1917, until Soviet authorities banned adat in the early 1930s and replaced it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dissolution-of-the-soviet-union">dissolution of the Soviet Union</a> it was revived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> in the 1990s. Adat is still enforced as personal law in the courts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a>, Malaysia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> in certain respects.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Adat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Islamic law was administered by a chief judge, the qadi malik al-adil, and the court took pride in combining Islamic law with custom; later tradition attributed Aceh's customary constitution, the <b>Adat</b> Meukuta Alam, to Iskandar Muda.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aceh-sultanate">Aceh Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Customary law, the <b>adat</b>, was applied by mediators and courts of respected men, while Islamic law, the sharia, gained ground with the spread of Islam.</blockquote><a href="/empire/circassia">Circassia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Law was plural: European codes applied to Europeans, while for Indonesians customary law (<b>adat</b>), studied by Cornelis van Vollenhoven and his school at Leiden, was recognised alongside Islamic law in family matters.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Justice lay with the biy, elders respected for their eloquence and knowledge of customary law (<b>adat</b>).</blockquote><a href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Law combined customary (<b>adat</b>) practice with royal decrees and Indian-derived legal codes, recorded in inscriptions and law books, while religious officials for Shaivite and Buddhist affairs had a part in resolving disputes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Malacca was governed by written law, the Undang-Undang Melaka, also called the Hukum Kanun Melaka, and the Maritime Laws, shaped by Hindu-Buddhist tradition, Islam and indigenous <b>adat</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malacca-sultanate">Malacca Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
